1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:01,860
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:05,980
I equipped this lazy converter build with voltage feedback.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:11,200
Now it stably holds 238V at the output.

4
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:15,120
The feedback is also done quite simply.

5
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:16,840
Two additional chips.

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:24,780
One of them is a PC817 opticoupler, the other a TL431 adjustable shunt regulator.

7
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:28,300
For resistors, one capacitor.

8
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:30,260
That's the whole feedback.

9
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:37,500
So this converter now lets you supply not only 12V at the input, but also up to 20V inclusive.

10
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:40,620
In any voltage range of that order.

11
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:44,820
And the output voltage is stabilized.

12
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:49,700
Now we'll test it and look at the feedback circuit in more detail.

13
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:54,820
Now we'll test the voltage feedback trip point.

14
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:58,400
A multimeter is connected to the output.

15
00:00:58,740 --> 00:00:59,960
The lamp isn't connected.

16
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:03,080
Applying 12V power.

17
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:06,880
You can hear the converter trip point.

18
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:08,120
It starts to buzz.

19
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:12,020
That's the sound.

20
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:15,180
237V.

21
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:18,800
Connect the lamp.

22
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:22,120
You see there's a slight drop on the lamp.

23
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:23,700
218V.

24
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:29,620
The output voltage went away from the feedback trip point, and the converter runs in normal mode.

25
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:31,720
No sound at all.

26
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:38,640
With voltage feedback present, we can supply a higher voltage at the input.

27
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:42,580
Up to 20V, not worth going higher.

28
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:45,460
Now applying 14V.

29
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:51,180
You see the voltage is within norm and the feedback trip sound appeared.

30
00:01:53,940 --> 00:02:00,320
The sound comes from the step-up transformer, since the chip now drives the switches in pulse bursts mode.

31
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:06,710
If we reduce, the sound disappears.

32
00:02:07,610 --> 00:02:08,690
Feedback fully works.

33
00:02:09,530 --> 00:02:11,470
About what you can connect to this converter.

34
00:02:13,110 --> 00:02:18,070
You can connect all modern devices that have a transformer-less inverter at the input.

35
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:26,520
That is, mobile phone chargers, laptop chargers, and similar devices.

36
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:32,400
You can't connect devices that have a step-down transformer at the input.

37
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:38,760
Such power supplies, their weight is significantly higher than an inverter-type supply.

38
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:44,080
You also can't connect asynchronous motors here.

39
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:49,060
Most often we encounter them in everyday life and fans, for example.

40
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:53,220
And brush motors that are here will work freely.

41
00:02:54,600 --> 00:03:00,680
That is, various kinds of small drills at 220V with power up to 200W.

42
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:02,980
This system will freely power them.

43
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:10,460
To add voltage feedback to our circuit, we use a property of the IR2153 chip.

44
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:17,580
If you apply logical zero to pin 3 of this chip, the chip stops generating on both channels.

45
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:25,130
For this, I went with a variant using a galvanically isolated optocoupler, the widespread PC817.

46
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:31,420
I soldered it into the circuit per its pinout.

47
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:38,460
Pins 1 and 2 are the infrared LED built into the optocoupler.

48
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:42,760
Pins 3 and 4 are the phototransistor.

49
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:46,420
The optocoupler is this small part.

50
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:48,360
Here it is with four pins.

51
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:55,900
On the top of the case, there's a dot, which marks pin numbering.

52
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:01,160
This is pin 1.

53
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:08,640
The pins, if you look starting from the dot, in this case it's now at the upper left corner, are counted counterclockwise.

54
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:12,120
First, second, third, and fourth.

55
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:16,660
On the schematic, the optocoupler is shown lying with legs up.

56
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:20,940
That orientation matches its pinout.

57
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:24,840
So, solder the optocoupler into the circuit.

58
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:28,500
Here you can see I installed it on the board.

59
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:31,320
And check whether the optocoupler works.

60
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:37,280
For this we apply power to the converter, first soldering a small load to it.

61
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:42,820
In this case, a 15W lamp.

62
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:45,640
Apply power.

63
00:04:48,910 --> 00:04:50,530
You see, the lamp lit.

64
00:04:50,530 --> 00:04:55,270
Now we need to solder a wire to pin 2 of the optocoupler.

65
00:04:57,630 --> 00:04:58,410
There it is.

66
00:04:59,490 --> 00:05:03,490
And to pin 1 of the optocoupler, a 91 ohms current limiting resistor.

67
00:05:04,610 --> 00:05:06,430
And a 1.5V battery.

68
00:05:07,670 --> 00:05:10,010
Minus from the battery goes to pin 2.

69
00:05:10,870 --> 00:05:12,110
Plus to the resistor.

70
00:05:13,270 --> 00:05:15,510
And watch, I connect the battery.

71
00:05:15,510 --> 00:05:24,880
And when the battery plus is connected to the resistor, the chip stops generation, the load goes out.

72
00:05:27,900 --> 00:05:31,420
This experiment shows the optocoupler is in working order.

73
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:42,700
If you have a lab signal generator on hand, you can watch how the converter triggers under optocoupler control with a square signal applied to the input, the generator common.

74
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:48,600
Connects to pin 2, the signal 1 to pin 1.

75
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:50,800
Also through a current limiting resistor.

76
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:56,160
The input potential should be no more than 5V.

77
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:00,780
1.5 to 2V is enough to trigger the optocoupler easily.

78
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:03,400
Equivalent to what we did with the battery.

79
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:06,520
I'm applying a square signal at 10 hertz.

80
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:10,880
You see the circuit responds great.

81
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:14,900
Reducing frequency.

82
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:17,280
5 hertz.

83
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:25,380
Understand, those who work with electronics, on this principle you can already form chip operation in burst mode.

84
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:31,560
1 hertz frequency.

85
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:40,500
If you apply, say, mains frequency, here I smoothly raise it, now 25 hertz.

86
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:46,240
50.

87
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:53,420
You can hear, when there will be a way to supply this, that this step-up transformer hums at 50 hertz mains frequency.

88
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:58,600
Next, we need to introduce another element into the circuit.

89
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:01,140
The TL431 chip.

90
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:04,520
Graphically it's shown as a zener.

91
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:08,160
With a branch you get an adjustable shunt regulator system.

92
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:11,520
The pinout is shown under the graphic.

93
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:15,500
The part itself is also very small.

94
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:17,600
Here it is.

95
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:20,800
Three pins on the part.

96
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:25,460
Pins are counted left to right if you read the chip name straight on.

97
00:07:30,140 --> 00:07:31,080
What's this for?

98
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:43,000
Earlier I shared a schematic of another converter, and you can see that here, by feedback, the master chip is controlled directly through the PC817 opticoupler by dividing.

99
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:47,880
Voltage on a series resistor stack.

100
00:07:51,090 --> 00:08:01,790
You can't control it that way in this circuit, because if the LED's glow has different intensity, the phototransistor, since it's bipolar, will open with different conductivity levels.

101
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:07,340
Open with various degrees of conductivity.

102
00:08:27,530 --> 00:08:32,810
And if you measure it, play with the opticoupler, you'll see the transistor's resistance changes.

103
00:08:33,910 --> 00:08:40,130
And for this chip you need at least 200 ohms of resistance to reliably short pin three to the common wire.

104
00:08:43,100 --> 00:08:50,800
That's exactly why an additional sensitive element, the TL431, was added, which controls this opticoupler.

105
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:55,640
The TL431 is a pretty elderly chip for us.

106
00:08:57,180 --> 00:09:01,480
Made in the late 1970s, but still widely used to this day.

107
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:10,960
Its advantages are operating voltage up to 36V and the main one, being able to set the chip's trip points.

108
00:09:13,890 --> 00:09:18,210
Inside there's a reference power source of about 2.5V and a comparator.

109
00:09:20,890 --> 00:09:28,650
The comparator has stabilized 2.5V at one input, and the input from the external part, pin one, at the other.

110
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:33,750
The comparator compares the reference and the input voltage.

111
00:09:34,450 --> 00:09:41,450
If the input exceeds the reference, the chip triggers, and, graphically speaking, the zener breaks down, i.e.

112
00:09:41,490 --> 00:09:42,990
the chip starts to conduct.

113
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:46,250
I picked the divider on the resistors.

114
00:09:46,970 --> 00:09:51,230
One what resistors of 4.6kΩ and 390kΩ.

115
00:09:52,270 --> 00:09:59,950
With these resistor values, if you take its leads already from the converter output, the voltage protection trips at 238V.

116
00:10:05,570 --> 00:10:11,930
That is, by changing the values of the 4.6kΩ resistor, you can set the converter's trip point.

117
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:20,590
You mustn't forget to put this capacitor of at least 10nF, ceramic, between pins 3 and 1.

118
00:10:21,710 --> 00:10:26,870
Otherwise, false feedback triggers are possible from some transient processes in the transformer.

119
00:10:27,670 --> 00:10:32,510
For example, if there are needle-like voltage spikes, the chip will trigger earlier than needed.

120
00:10:34,030 --> 00:10:35,510
Capacitor is mandatory.

121
00:10:35,890 --> 00:10:41,630
And this 1kΩ resistor placed in parallel with the optocoupler's LED.

122
00:10:43,570 --> 00:10:50,270
This resistor also keeps the LED from glowing when the TL431 chip is approaching its trip point.

123
00:10:55,050 --> 00:10:56,730
Limiting resistor 150Ω.

124
00:10:58,870 --> 00:11:04,230
I searched for feedbacks of this kind online at first, but interestingly, found nothing.

125
00:11:04,930 --> 00:11:09,210
Lots of such solutions for low voltage, but with high voltage, I had to fiddle.

126
00:11:13,320 --> 00:11:19,400
I picked the divider through long practice, and changed various circuit variants until it started working stably.

127
00:11:21,740 --> 00:11:28,220
Many parts for this converter can be borrowed from broken power supplies, for example, from computer power supplies.

128
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,700
You can buy them cheap, or you may be given them for free at service centers.

129
00:11:39,550 --> 00:11:45,150
In particular, you can see on the board there's a PC817 optocoupler and this chip.

130
00:11:49,020 --> 00:11:49,760
A small TL431.

131
00:11:53,520 --> 00:11:56,160
Also heat sinks you can mount transistors on.

132
00:11:59,110 --> 00:12:03,490
Thermal insulating gaskets under these transistors, you can find them all here.

133
00:12:05,610 --> 00:12:07,370
And this is also quite suitable.

134
00:12:08,810 --> 00:12:10,810
You can disassemble this transformer.

135
00:12:12,490 --> 00:12:21,650
I unsoldered it from the board, but usually either heat it with a heat gun until the glue melts and the halves separate, or boil it in water until it falls apart.

136
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:31,060
After this it's all unwound, and you win your own winding to the voltages you want.

137
00:12:32,660 --> 00:12:36,180
So this converter can be very cheap if you make it from salvage.

138
00:12:40,370 --> 00:12:49,750
If this converter will be used, for example, for lighting when power is out, then I'd suggest in this case putting some 20A diode at the input, any brand.

139
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:55,000
Whatever you find.

140
00:12:57,080 --> 00:12:59,460
Voltage rating not less than 100V.

141
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:05,400
So in poor lighting conditions you don't mix up the power inputs.

142
00:13:07,980 --> 00:13:15,600
Otherwise, if you mix them up, the circuit will of course go bad immediately, the chip will fail, and possibly one of the switches will blow.

143
00:13:17,420 --> 00:13:21,120
If it's stationary and constantly connected, you can skip the diode.

144
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:25,520
But anyone who wants can also equip the input with a fuse.

145
00:13:29,460 --> 00:13:32,180
That is, at your discretion you can upgrade this circuit.

146
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:35,980
Also on this principle, I applied this feedback by voltage.

147
00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:46,900
I need an operating voltage point such that, if voltage feedback is built, you can supply not just 12V but up to 20V inclusive at the input, and with that it'll.

148
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:49,100
Stably hold output voltage.

149
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:57,280
You can even pocket 2 9V, chronotype, batteries, connect them in series, and supply 18V at the input.

150
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:00,760
And you'll have 220V at the output.

151
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:03,440
Always able to charge your phone.

152
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:09,240
The whole circuit is that flexible.

153
00:14:11,800 --> 00:14:14,920
Anyone interested can also assemble current feedback.

154
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:20,040
That is, if you want the chip to go into protection on output shorting.

155
00:14:20,680 --> 00:14:27,820
For this you'll need to change the divider resistor, wind a small toroidal transformer, the so-called current transformer.

156
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:35,900
You can hang it at this common point with the power plus to the center of the primary winding.

157
00:14:39,830 --> 00:14:45,530
And by playing with its turn count, bring the operating voltage point to somewhere above 2.6V.

158
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:51,600
Say if this converter works on a 200V load.

159
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:58,730
And feed this output from the current sensor to a small diode bridge.

160
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:02,920
After this put a cap.

161
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:07,760
Approximately, you'll have a circuit analogous to this one shown.

162
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:15,140
A diode bridge, a cap, and you can end up with a quite working system that protects the converter from short voltage.

163
00:15:15,560 --> 00:15:17,780
That is, from a short circuit at its output.

164
00:15:22,580 --> 00:15:24,760
Good luck to everyone with your experiments.

165
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:25,840
All the best!

